\subsection{MLP Projector: Graded Degradation}
\label{sec:openvla_results}

Under random token removal, OpenVLA degrades gracefully across all retention ratios: 69.4\% SR at $k{=}0.95$ ($-4.6$pp from the 74.0\% baseline), 62.0\% at $k{=}0.90$, 49.0\% at $k{=}0.70$, 37.4\% at $k{=}0.50$, and 20.6\% at $k{=}0.30$ (Figure~\ref{fig:results_combined}a; full results with CIs in Table~\ref{tab:openvla_full}, Appendix). At $k{=}0.30$, two tasks (cream\_cheese, butter) reach 0\% SR, but eight retain non-zero performance: degradation is graded, not cliff-edge. Per-task breakdowns are in Appendix~\ref{app:per_task}. Random token removal provides a conservative lower bound on architectural resilience; learned scoring methods would likely yield better retention.

OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} strengthens the connector-centric explanation. OFT replaces OpenVLA's autoregressive action head with an optimized fine-tuning strategy but retains the same 3-layer MLP connector. Its attention entropy (0.667) is within 1\% of standard OpenVLA (0.661), which confirms that the connector determines the attention structure governing token importance, independent of the action head. Results from EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla} corroborate the graded pattern on a related architecture: CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact}, which also uses an MLP projector, retains 74.2\% SR (from 74.8\% baseline) under 78\% token pruning.

\subsection{Linear Projection: From Immune to Threshold-Graded}
\label{sec:linear_results}

$\pi_0$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B parameters, 256 tokens, linear projection) uses a flow matching action head~\cite{lipman2023flow}; we evaluate pruning robustness via offline normalized L1 action loss rather than LIBERO success rate.\footnote{Offline action loss measures single-step prediction quality; closed-loop evaluation, where errors compound, may reveal additional degradation. We report offline results as preliminary evidence pending live evaluation. X-VLA's offline-to-closed-loop gap (offline near-immune at $k{=}0.3$, closed-loop cliff at $k{=}0.7$; Section~\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}) underscores the need for live verification.} At $k{=}0.1$ (retaining only 10\% of tokens), the normalized loss is 0.976, a 2.4\% \emph{improvement} over the unpruned baseline ($\mathcal{L}_1 < 1.0$ indicates lower loss than the full-token reference). At $k{=}0.5$ the loss remains 0.983. Token removal at these ratios does not degrade offline action prediction quality, though closed-loop validation is pending.

X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M parameters, 50 tokens) is nominally a linear projection model but routes vision tokens through a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} before the LLM, creating a more complex effective pipeline. The baseline achieves 99.2\% SR (496/500). At $k{=}0.95$ SR remains 99.0\% ($-0.2$pp), and at $k{=}0.9$ it holds at 97.6\% ($-1.6$pp). However, at $k{=}0.7$ SR drops to 56.2\% (281/500), a 43-percentage-point cliff from $k{=}0.9$. X-VLA thus exhibits a threshold-graded pattern: robust under mild pruning ($k \geq 0.9$), then a sharp onset of degradation when 30\% of tokens are removed. This pattern differs from both $\pi_0$'s offline immunity and OpenVLA's smooth degradation (49.0\% at $k{=}0.7$). The BART encoder introduces cross-token mixing that creates partial dependencies (CV $= 0.151$, between $\pi_0$'s 0.038 and OpenVLA's 0.215), explaining the intermediate onset point.

The $\pi_0$--X-VLA contrast within the nominal ``linear projection'' class demonstrates that the connector label alone is insufficient: the effective vision-to-LLM pipeline complexity determines pruning tolerance. $\pi_0$'s single linear layer preserves full token independence (CV $= 0.038$), while X-VLA's BART encoder introduces enough cross-token structure to shift the degradation onset inward.

The controlled comparison between $\pi_0$ and OpenVLA remains the strongest evidence for connector-determined compressibility. Both use 256 vision tokens from a SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} encoder; they differ only in the connector (linear projection vs.\ 3-layer MLP). At $k{=}0.1$, $\pi_0$ improves by 2.4\% while OpenVLA degrades by 61\%. With token count, encoder architecture, and pruning protocol held constant, the 63-percentage-point gap is attributable to the connector alone.

\subsection{VTR: Re-Encoding Overcomes Cliff-Edge}
\label{sec:smart_compress_results}

To test whether learned re-encoding can overcome the pixel-shuffle cliff-edge, we insert a 22.2M-parameter cross-attention module (Vision Token Re-encoder, VTR) between the vision connector and the LLM backbone (details in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}). VTR compresses 128 input tokens to $M$ output tokens. Both VTR and the baseline train from the same SmolVLA-Base checkpoint for 100K steps with identical hyperparameters. An extended training control (same procedure without the compressor) achieves 45.0\% SR ($p > 0.9$ vs.\ the 44.8\% baseline), which confirms the gain is architectural, not from additional optimization steps.\footnote{The extended baseline also collapses to 0\% SR under pruning ($k{=}0.50$, random, true removal), which confirms that additional training does not mitigate the pixel-shuffle vulnerability.}

