\section{Analysis and Discussion}
\label{sec:analysis}

\subsection{Effective Pipeline Complexity Determines Token Independence}
\label{sec:pixel_shuffle}

A controlled comparison between $\pi_0$ and OpenVLA isolates the vision connector as the causal factor behind compressibility differences. Both models process 256 vision tokens from SigLIP encoders, yet at $k{=}0.1$, $\pi_0$'s offline action loss improves by 2.4\% while OpenVLA's closed-loop SR degrades by 61\%. This 63 percentage-point gap, with token count and encoder held constant, can only be attributed to the connector. We note that $\pi_0$'s result is offline; X-VLA's offline-to-closed-loop divergence (Section~\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}) cautions against treating offline immunity as definitive.

The vision-to-LLM pipeline creates fundamentally different token structures depending on its effective complexity. Pixel-shuffle connectors (SmolVLA) apply $4 \times 4$ spatial folding: each of 128 tokens encodes 16 patches via channel concatenation (Figure~\ref{fig:pixel_shuffle}). Removing any token eliminates an entire receptive field with no redundancy from neighbors. The folding is fixed and non-learned, so no downstream layer can recover discarded information.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.85\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_pixel_shuffle.pdf}
\caption{Pixel-shuffle folding: each $4{\times}4$ block of SigLIP patches is channel-concatenated into one token. Removing any token eliminates its receptive field (gray) with no redundancy.}
\label{fig:pixel_shuffle}
\end{figure}

Pure linear projection connectors ($\pi_0$) apply a single linear layer that maps each patch independently. No cross-token interaction occurs during projection, so tokens are genuinely independent. Removing one token loses approximately $1/N$ of the visual field, and the loss is spatially uniform.

X-VLA complicates this picture. Its connector is nominally a linear projection, but vision tokens subsequently pass through a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart} before reaching the LLM. The BART encoder's self-attention layers introduce cross-token mixing that creates partial dependencies: X-VLA's CV (0.151) is four times higher than $\pi_0$'s (0.038), and its attention entropy (0.614) is substantially lower. This explains X-VLA's threshold-graded pattern: the BART encoder differentiates tokens enough that removing 30\% crosses a threshold, but not enough to cause degradation at 10\% removal. The nominal connector label (``linear projection'') is insufficient; the effective pipeline complexity---including any post-connector processing---determines pruning tolerance.

MLP projectors (OpenVLA, OFT) process each patch through a 3-layer MLP with GELU activations. Per-patch processing preserves spatial locality, but non-linear transformations create partial dependencies between each projected representation and the broader feature space. The result is an intermediate regime: tokens carry differentiated information without the rigid spatial entanglement of pixel-shuffle.

The OFT variant confirms that the action head does not determine attention structure. OFT and OpenVLA share the same MLP connector but differ in their action decoding mechanism. Their attention entropy values are nearly identical (0.667 and 0.661, respectively), consistent with the connector, not the action head, controlling how information is distributed across vision tokens.

\subsection{Token Importance Heterogeneity as Diagnostic}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

The coefficient of variation (CV) of token importance, measured as the variation in L2 norms across vision tokens, captures the heterogeneity that determines pruning tolerance. CV forms a continuous spectrum across the four measured models: $\pi_0$ CV $= 0.038$ (tokens carry near-equal importance), X-VLA CV $= 0.151$ (mild differentiation), OpenVLA CV $= 0.215$ (moderate differentiation), SmolVLA CV $= 0.314$ (high heterogeneity). This ordering predicts not just the class of degradation but the onset point: $\pi_0$ (CV $= 0.038$) is immune through $k{=}0.1$, X-VLA (CV $= 0.151$) is robust until $k{=}0.7$ then cliffs, OpenVLA (CV $= 0.215$) degrades from $k{=}0.95$, and SmolVLA (CV $= 0.314$) collapses at $k{=}0.95$.

Attention entropy provides the mechanistic explanation for why CV predicts pruning behavior. $\pi_0$ attention entropy is 0.9999, near-maximally uniform across all 18 layers (range 0.931--0.983). When all tokens contribute equally, any subset preserves a proportional share of the total information, and the model can redistribute attention without loss. Low CV combined with high entropy means tokens are interchangeable: removing any subset is equivalent to uniform downsampling.

SmolVLA presents an apparent paradox. Its attention entropy is also high (0.952, range 0.93--0.98, spread 0.05), yet pruning is catastrophic. The resolution lies in the combination of high entropy with high CV: tokens are uniformly attended (the model treats them all as necessary) but carry heterogeneous spatial content due to pixel-shuffle folding. The model cannot selectively attend to a subset because all tokens are equally demanded, yet the tokens themselves are not interchangeable. This ``differentiated but entangled'' pattern is the signature of pixel-shuffle connectors: uniform attention over non-uniform content.

X-VLA occupies a revealing intermediate position. Its CV ($0.151$) falls between $\pi_0$ and OpenVLA, and its attention entropy ($0.614$) is the lowest among the four models. The 12-layer BART encoder introduces cross-token mixing that partially differentiates tokens, creating enough heterogeneity to trigger a cliff at $k{=}0.7$ but not enough to cause degradation at $k{=}0.9$. X-VLA demonstrates that CV predicts the \emph{onset point} of degradation, not merely a categorical class.

OpenVLA occupies the graded regime. Its overall entropy is 0.661, with progressive sharpening from 0.98 in layer 0 to 0.48 in layer 29 (spread 0.50; Figure~\ref{fig:entropy_layers} and Appendix~\ref{app:entropy_details}). The model learns to concentrate attention on informative tokens across processing depth. Moderate CV (0.215) means tokens are partially differentiated: pruning removes information, but the remaining tokens carry sufficient signal for graded rather than catastrophic degradation. OFT attention entropy (0.667) is nearly identical to OpenVLA (0.661), confirming that the action head is irrelevant to the attention structure.

Token importance heterogeneity (CV) has practical value as a diagnostic because it requires only a forward pass on a sample of task-relevant frames. Four models (with OFT as a connector-controlled variant) now span the full CV spectrum from 0.038 to 0.314, and the CV ordering correctly predicts both the degradation class and onset across all cases.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_entropy_layers.pdf}
\caption{Per-layer attention entropy ratio for SmolVLA (16 layers), OpenVLA (32 layers), and $\pi_0$ (18 layers). SmolVLA and $\pi_0$ maintain near-uniform attention throughout, while OpenVLA progressively sharpens from 0.98 to 0.48. Despite similar entropy, SmolVLA and $\pi_0$ exhibit opposite pruning outcomes due to token importance heterogeneity (CV $= 0.314$ vs.\ $0.038$). X-VLA (entropy $= 0.614$, CV $= 0.151$) occupies an intermediate position, consistent with its threshold-graded pattern (onset at $k{=}0.7$).}
\label{fig:entropy_layers}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Why Re-Encoding Succeeds Where Deletion Fails}
\label{sec:learned_compression}

Two architecturally distinct re-encoding modules, cross-attention pooling (22.2M parameters) and a parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters), both recover $\sim$60--63\% SR on SmolVLA where all deletion methods yield 0\% (3-seed means: cross-attention $60.1 \pm 1.3$, MLP $63.4 \pm 2.6$; two-sample $t$-test $p = 0.15$). The absence of a statistically significant difference between architectures identifies the shared property, learned re-encoding of the full token set, as the operative factor.

The $M$-curve provides direct evidence that re-encoding preserves information regardless of compression ratio. Performance is flat across a wide range: $M{=}16$ yields $56.9 \pm 1.3$\% and $M{=}128$ yields $58.0 \pm 3.5$\%, with intermediate values of 60.1\% ($M{=}32$), 62.0\% ($M{=}64$), and 54.5\% ($M{=}96$). The Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR) redistributes information into fewer tokens without destroying it, even at $8\times$ compression ($M{=}16$, retaining 12.5\% of tokens). This stands in sharp contrast to hard pruning, where removing 5\% of pixel-shuffle tokens causes complete collapse.

The $M{=}128$ passthrough condition, where the re-encoder outputs the same number of tokens as the input, achieves $\sim$58\% SR versus a 44.8\% baseline. This 13-point gain from re-encoding alone, without any compression, shows that the learned module adds value through feature reorganization. An extended training control (100K additional steps without the compressor, 45.0\% SR) confirms that the gain is architectural rather than a consequence of additional optimization.

The mechanism is straightforward. Hard pruning applies a binary mask: selected tokens pass through, the rest are discarded. Re-encoding preserves access to all 128 source tokens through learned transformations (weighted summation for cross-attention, feed-forward mapping for MLP). The learned module breaks the rigid spatial entanglement of pixel-shuffle tokens by integrating information across the original $4 \times 4$ receptive field boundaries. VTR is currently evaluated on SmolVLA only; whether re-encoding provides comparable gains for other connector types remains untested.

\subsection{Offline Metrics Underestimate Closed-Loop Degradation}
\label{sec:metric_disconnect}

Offline evaluation metrics underestimate the severity of pruning-induced failure and miss task-level heterogeneity. The aggregate $3.4\times$ action error increase (Table~\ref{tab:offline_action_error}, Appendix~\ref{app:offline_error}) is a clear negative signal, but per-step metrics do not distinguish moderate perturbations (position errors of 0.20--0.30 per axis) from catastrophic events (complete gripper inversion, L2 $= 1.02$) that individually terminate an episode. In closed-loop execution, a single catastrophic action pushes the robot off-trajectory, subsequent observations diverge from training data, and recovery becomes impossible. These events are rare enough to be diluted in per-step averages but occur in every pruned episode we tested.

X-VLA provides a concrete example of this disconnect. At $k{=}0.3$, X-VLA's offline normalized action loss is 1.0014---near the unpruned baseline, suggesting negligible degradation. Yet at $k{=}0.7$ (a less aggressive pruning ratio), closed-loop SR drops to 56.2\%, a 43pp cliff. Offline metrics register the average perturbation but not the task-level failures that dominate closed-loop outcomes. Offline evaluation remains useful for directional signals (e.g., $\pi_0$'s improving loss at $k{=}0.1$ suggests genuine robustness), but closed-loop evaluation is essential for validating any VLA compression method.

\subsection{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

All closed-loop experiments use the LIBERO simulation benchmark, which does not capture real-world deployment conditions such as sensor noise, calibration drift, and physical contact dynamics. Scale confounding is partially addressed: our taxonomy spans five models from 450M to 7B parameters, and $\pi_0$ (3.3B) is immune while OpenVLA (7B) is vulnerable, which suggests scale alone does not explain the observed differences. A matched-scale experiment remains the ideal control.

$\pi_0$ evaluation is currently offline only; closed-loop results may reveal additional degradation not captured by single-step loss, as demonstrated by X-VLA's offline-to-closed-loop gap (Section~\ref{sec:metric_disconnect}). X-VLA closed-loop evaluation covers $k \geq 0.7$; lower retention ratios remain untested. OpenVLA VTR experiments are also pending.

VTR is evaluated on SmolVLA only; generalization of re-encoding to other connector types is untested. Re-encoding on the well-trained official SmolVLA checkpoint yields 53.4\% SR, below the 59.2\% uncompressed baseline. The gain on our fine-tuned model (44.8\% $\to$ 60.1\%) is larger, consistent with re-encoding providing the most benefit when baseline features are undertrained.

